---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { loadResults } from '../lib/data.js';
const r = loadResults();
const p = r.pooled;
---
<Base title="Add your course to the study" description="How to contribute autonomous mowing data to the Auburn turfgrass study.">
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open call &middot; {p.sites} of a target 20 courses</p>
      <h1>We need 10 to 20 more courses.</h1>
      <p>
        Three sites show a direction. Twenty sites would let a superintendent see results from
        courses that look like theirs — same climate, same budget tier, same acreage — instead of
        extrapolating from someone else&rsquo;s.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-title">What participating costs you</h2>
      <table class="spec">
        <caption>One mowing session</caption>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Time on your end</th><td>One morning. Someone with a clipboard records start and finish times.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>What gets recorded</th><td>Each fairway mowed, transport between them, setup, washdown — and separately, what the operator did while the machine ran.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Also useful</th><td>The same fairways mowed manually on a different day, so there is a baseline to compare against.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>What we need from the office</th><td>Fairway acreage, loaded hourly wage, fuel used, and mower model. Acreage is the one we most often don't get.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:34px">What you get back</h2>
      <table class="spec">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Your own numbers</th><td>A written summary of your course's results before anything is published.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Comparison</th><td>Where your operation sits against every other participating site.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Review rights</th><td>You see your site page before it goes live. If you want something removed, it comes out.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-title">Anonymity</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">{r.anonymization}</p>
      <div class="gaps">
        <h3>Published for every site</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Facility type — private club, resort course, daily fee, municipal</li>
          <li>Region — Southeast US, Mountain West, Desert Southwest</li>
          <li>Turf system — warm-season, cool-season, overseeded</li>
          <li>Acreage, times, fuel, and wage figures</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="gaps" style="margin-top:14px">
        <h3>Never published</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Course or club name</li>
          <li>Superintendent or staff names</li>
          <li>City, state, or any location narrower than region</li>
          <li>Anything you ask us to withhold</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-title">How to start</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Email the turfgrass program at Auburn University and ask for the mowing study data
        collection packet. You will get three spreadsheets and a two-page protocol. Fill in what
        you can — a partial submission is far more useful than none, and an empty cell is more
        useful than a guess.
      </p>
      <p class="section-lede">
        If you run a platform other than the one measured here — a fully autonomous rough mower,
        a different fairway unit — we especially want your data. Rough mowing is the largest
        acreage on most courses and currently has no measurements at all.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
